PCI DSS stands for Payment Card Industry Data Security Standard. 
It is a global security standard designed to ensure that all companies that accept, process, store, or transmit credit card information maintain a secure environment.

✅ Steps to Achieve PCI DSS Compliance in Snowflake

1. Choose the Right Edition
    Use the Business Critical (or higher) Snowflake edition:
    Enables Tri-Secret Secure, auto-encryption, and HIPAA/PCI workloads.
    Contact Snowflake sales/support to enable PCI-specific controls.
2. Restrict and Monitor Access (Req 7, 8, 10)
🔐 Identity & Access Management:
    Integrate with SSO/SAML (e.g., Okta, Azure AD).
    Enforce Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA).
    Use RBAC (Role-Based Access Control):
3. Encrypt All Data (Req 3, 4)
    Data at rest: Automatically encrypted by Snowflake using AES-256.
    Data in transit: Encrypted using TLS 1.2+.
    Enable Tri-Secret Secure (optional but useful for enhanced key control).
4. Minimize Data Retention (Req 3.1)
    Store only the last 4 digits of card numbers unless absolutely required.
    Use data masking, tokenization, or external vaults for full PAN data.

✅ What is GDPR?
GDPR stands for General Data Protection Regulation, a European Union (EU) law effective since May 25, 2018, designed to:
Protect the privacy and personal data of EU citizens.
Give individuals more control over their data.
Require organizations to be transparent, secure, and accountable in their data processing.
It applies to any organization worldwide that collects or processes personal data of individuals in the EU or EEA—not just EU-based businesses.

